      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "clickSelector",
          "selector": "#bs-toolbar [data-panel=\"employees\"]"
        },
        {
          "type": "waitForSelector",
          "selector": "#bs-employee-panel [data-role=\"driller\"]"
        },
        {
          "type": "clickSelector",
          "selector": "#bs-employee-panel [data-role=\"driller\"]"
        }
      ],
      "role": "player",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "employeeCount": 1,
          "cash": 49000
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "employee assign_skill 1 skill:driving.excavator level:1",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "employee assign_skill 1 skill:driving.excavator level:1"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Test-only bootstrap, not a player action: hiring already grants the Rookie starting qualification (ROLE_STARTING_QUALIFICATION in Employee.ts, same for the UI hire button and this console command), so this only raises the level for the scenario's needs. No role tag -- there is no UI control for this and none should exist; the player-facing way to raise a skill is `employee train` at a training building. Command mode keeps running it unchanged; interaction mode keeps it as an unmarked (legacy) command step. (Also fixes a Finding #15-class bug: the original step used `id:1` for the employee id, but assign_skill's id argument is positional, not named -- it silently failed with the Usage message.)",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "proficiencyTotal": 2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "employee list",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "employee list"
        }
      ],
      "role": "observe"
    },
    {
      "command": "employee dispatch 1 x:15 z:15 skill:driving.excavator",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "employee dispatch 1 x:15 z:15 skill:driving.excavator"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Test-only bootstrap, not a player action: `employee dispatch` exists (per its own doc comment in employees.ts) purely so console/scenario driving can put an employee to genuine, ticksWorked-incrementing work without a full drill/haul pipeline -- there is no UI control for it and none should exist. Repeated 40 times here (queued back to back before any tick, all targeted at employee #1) so the driller has continuous excavator work queued through the full 700-tick budget below; each dispatched task only lasts ~20-45 ticks depending on proficiency and fatigue, so a single dispatch would leave the employee idle (and gaining no XP -- XP only accrues via tickTaskProgress while a task is actively in progress) for most of the 700 ticks. Verified via a real dump that 40 is enough to keep the driller continuously busy through tick 700 with none left over."
    },
